"[2511.09363] BarrierBench: Evaluating Large Language Models for Safety Verification in Dynamical Systems (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09363)\nciteturn5979view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09363\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 167\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 16 Apr 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:BarrierBench: Evaluating Large Language Models for Safety Verification in Dynamical Systems\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Ali Taheri , cite7†Alireza Taban , cite8†Sadegh Soudjani , cite9†Ashutosh Trivedi L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled BarrierBench: Evaluating Large Language Models for Safety Verification in Dynamical Systems, by Ali Taheri and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Safety verification of dynamical systems via barrier certificates is essential for ensuring correctness in autonomous applications. Synthesizing these certificates involves discovering mathematical functions with current methods suffering from poor scalability, dependence on carefully designed templates, and exhaustive or incremental function-space searches.\nL17: They also demand substantial manual expertise--selecting templates, solvers, and hyperparameters, and designing sampling strategies--requiring both theoretical and practical knowledge traditionally shared through linguistic reasoning rather than formalized methods.\nL18: > This motivates a key question: can such expert reasoning be captured and operationalized by language models? We address this by introducing an LLM-based agentic framework for barrier certificate synthesis. The framework uses natural language reasoning to propose, refine, and validate candidate certificates, integrating LLM-driven template discovery with SMT-based verification, and supporting barrier-controller co-synthesis to ensure consistency between safety certificates and controllers.\nL19: > To evaluate this capability, we introduce BarrierBench, a benchmark of 100 dynamical systems spanning linear, nonlinear, discrete-time, and continuous-time settings. Our experiments assess not only the effectiveness of LLM-guided barrier synthesis but also the utility of retrieval-augmented generation and agentic coordination strategies in improving its reliability and performance. Across these tasks, the framework achieves more than 90% success in generating valid certificates.\nL20: By releasing BarrierBench and the accompanying toolchain, we aim to establish a community testbed for advancing the integration of language-based reasoning with formal verification in dynamical systems.\nL21: > The benchmark is publicly available at cite12†this https URL†hycodev.com L22: Comments:  | 8th Annual Learning for Dynamics & Control Conference\nL23: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Systems and Control (eess.SY)\nL24: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.09363 [cs.AI]\nL25:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2511.09363v2 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL26:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09363†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL27: ## Submission history\nL28: \nL29: From: Ali Taheri [cite16†view email ]\nL30: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 14:23:49 UTC (281 KB)\nL31: [v2] Thu, 16 Apr 2026 14:24:01 UTC (288 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: \nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled BarrierBench: Evaluating Large Language Models for Safety Verification in Dynamical Systems, by Ali Taheri and 3 other authors\nL38: \nL39:   * cite10†View PDF L40:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L41:   * cite17†TeX